# Package set for the RECOVERY slot (p7).
#
# p7 is 450 MiB against the A/B slots' 850 MiB, and the A/B image already uses
# 669 MiB. So this list is not "the same thing, smaller" -- it is only what is
# needed to GET IN and REPAIR A SLOT, and nothing whatsoever else. Every entry
# below earns its bytes against that single job.
#
# The job, concretely:
#   1. come up on the network unattended        (systemd-networkd, resolved)
#   2. let someone in                           (openssh-server, + tailscale
#                                                installed from the upstream
#                                                tarball, not a package)
#   3. fetch an image                           (curl, ca-certificates)
#   4. write and verify a slot                  (coreutils, gzip, e2fsprogs)
#   5. move the boot pointer                    (libubootenv-tool)

systemd-sysv
udev
dbus
busybox
linux-base

# The U-Boot environment. Without this the recovery slot can write a slot but
# cannot make the box boot it, which is half a repair.
libubootenv-tool

# Network, unattended.
systemd-resolved
systemd-timesyncd
iproute2
iputils-ping
ca-certificates
curl

# Access.
openssh-server
openssh-client

# Repair.
e2fsprogs
fdisk
util-linux
mmc-utils

# Enough of an editor to fix a config by hand over serial at 115200 baud.
# nano rather than vim: a tenth of the size, and nobody needs modal editing
# while the box is down.
nano

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT INSTALLED (present in the A/B image, omitted here)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   initramfs-tools   The recovery slot boots with ramdisk_addr_r cleared and
#                     init=/init -- there is no initrd in this boot path at all,
#                     so building one wastes ~30 MiB and a lot of build time.
#                     This is the single biggest saving in the list.
#   vim, btop, jq     comfort, not repair.
#   tcpdump, iperf3   diagnosis of a WORKING box; this slot exists for a broken
#   fio               one, and you can install them at runtime if you must.
#   wireguard-tools   the recovery path out is ssh or tailscale, not a tunnel
#   nftables/iptables that has to be configured first.
